# Testing Conventions

## Stack
- **pytest** — test runner
- **pytest-asyncio** — async test support (`asyncio_mode = "auto"` in pyproject.toml)
- **httpx** — async HTTP client; also used for FastAPI test client via `ASGITransport`
- **pytest-mock** — mocking external services

## Running Tests
```bash
uv run pytest                     # all tests
uv run pytest tests/unit/         # unit only
uv run pytest tests/integration/  # integration only
uv run pytest -x                  # stop on first failure
```

## Directory Structure
```
tests/
  conftest.py            # Shared fixtures
  unit/
    services/            # intervals_service, trainerroad_service, etc.
    agents/              # Agent tools
  integration/           # FastAPI routes end-to-end
```

## Rules
- Never make real HTTP calls in tests — mock the HTTP layer
- Async tests just work with `asyncio_mode = "auto"` — no decorator needed
- Test naming: `test_<what>_<condition>_<expected_outcome>`
- Every test function must have a docstring explaining what it verifies and why
- Define shared fixtures in `tests/conftest.py` — one fixture per concern

## What to Test
- **Services:** API response parsing, error handling, edge cases (None values, empty responses)
- **Agent tools:** correct data passed to services, correct output shape
- **Routes:** HTTP status codes, correct template context, HTMX partial responses
- **Models:** Pydantic validation — required fields, constraints, defaults

## What Not to Test
- The Anthropic API itself — mock it
- intervals.icu or TrainerRoad APIs directly — mock them
- Jinja2 rendering internals — test that the route returns 200 and the right context
